ReplicationController  , ReplicaSet & Deployment 
  If a pod fails, there is no self healing properties. Thats when we started to place pods under controllers such as ReplicaSet , ReplicationController & deployment


ReplicationController  -  
Manages Pod Replication. Ensures desired number of pods are running. It completely works on the mentioned label in the pod. It is the older version of ReplicaSet 

Note- It is the original method of Self Healing property  
What is a ReplicationController?

* Ensures a specified number of pod replicas are running at any given time.
* Any pod with matching labels becomes part of the rc, regardless of how it was created.

 How to Label a Pod -   # k run nginx —image=nginx:1.19 -l app=nginx   
📌 What Is a ReplicationController?
A ReplicationController is a Kubernetes object that:
* ensures a specified number of Pod replicas are always running
* automatically replaces Pods that crash or are deleted
* ensures the system always matches the user’s desired state


📌 Why ReplicationController is Deprecated (Practically)
Although still technically supported, the Kubernetes community has largely moved on because:
❌ Only supports equality-based selectors
Not flexible enough for modern deployments.
❌ No rolling updates
You would have to manually delete old Pods and apply new ones.
❌ Doesn’t support modern Kubernetes patterns
Deployments, ReplicaSets, StatefulSets, DaemonSets are preferred.
✔️ Deployments fully replace RC functionality
Deployments:
* manage ReplicaSets
* handle rollouts/rollbacks
* support surge strategies
* scale more efficiently
  
ReplicationSet  - 
  A ReplicaSet (RS) is a Kubernetes object that guarantees a stable set of replica Pods.
It makes sure that “N number of Pods are running, no matter what.” 
It Doesn’t support Rolling Update/ Rollback .  We have more options such as matchLabels / matchExpressions where we can use in/not In / Exists / DoesNotExist  features  
* ReplicaSet is an improved version of ReplicationController.
* It supports set-based selectors, providing more flexibility in managing pods.
* ReplicaSets are often managed by Deployments, which add advanced capabilities.


Deployment - 

A Deployment sits on top of ReplicaSet. It provides rollingUpdate/ rollback .  

Equality-Based vs Set-Based Selectors

Selector Type	Supported By	Description
Equality-Based	rc, rs, Deployment	Matches pods where the label key equals a specific value. (app: nginx)
Set-Based	rs, Deployment	Matches pods based on conditions (In, NotIn, Exists, DoesNotExist).



# kubectl annotate deployments.apps nginx-deployment kubernetes.io/change-cause="Upgraded nginx 1.19 to 1.20"  Important Kubernetes Deployment Commands
 	•	Create a deployment Imperatively: kubectl create deployment nginx-deployment --image=nginx:1.19
* List all deployments: kubectl get deployments
* Describe a deployment: kubectl describe deployment nginx-deployment
* Update deployment image: kubectl set image deployment/nginx-deployment nginx-container=nginx:1.20
* Scale deployment replicas: kubectl scale deployment/nginx-deployment --replicas=5
* View rollout history: kubectl rollout history deployment/nginx-deployment
* Annotate a deployment: kubectl annotate deployment nginx-deployment kubernetes.io/change-cause="Upgraded nginx 1.19 to 1.20"
* Rollback to a previous revision: kubectl rollout undo deployment/nginx-deployment --to-revision=1
* Edit a deployment: kubectl edit deployment <deployment_name>
* Get deployment manifest in YAML format: kubectl get deployment <deployment_name> -o yaml
* Delete a deployment: kubectl delete deployment nginx-deployment


5. Key Differences Between rc, rs, and Deployments

Feature	ReplicationController (rc)	ReplicaSet (rs)	Deployment
Selectors	Equality-based only	Equality and set-based	Equality and set-based
Updates	Manual	Manual	Automated (rolling updates)
Scaling	Manual or imperative	Manual or imperative	Declarative and automated
Use Case	Legacy workloads	Modern workloads	Advanced workloads with CI/CD

To delete a ReplicaSet/RC but keep its Pods running, you must orphan the Pods so they are not deleted along with the ReplicaSet/RC. -  
kubectl delete rs <rs-name> --cascade=orphan
kubectl delete rc <rc-name> --cascade=orphan
